= The Library

This library is in transition. The Polish January 1922 opening government and simplified parliament, November 1922 election with exact seats, December 1922 presidential succession, player party, population model, PPS factions, advisers and self-defence organization are implemented. Later governments, most other organizations, most cards and dated events remain an unfinished, mixed baseline.

@menu

- @government: Poland's opening constitutional arrangements
- @presidency: Presidential office and December 1922 elections
- @weimar_timeline: Timeline of the Weimar Republic before 1928
- @curr_gov: Current government and cabinet portfolios
- @demographics: Demographics
- @parties: Parties
- @public_opinion: Public opinion (voting intentions, not seats)
- @paramilitaries: Paramilitary groups
- @factions: Internal factions
- @advisers: PPS advisers
- @figures: Figures and charts
- @backSpecialScene: Exit library.

@government
go-to: menu

= Poland in January 1922

The March Constitution has been adopted, but its transitional arrangements preserve the **Sejm Ustawodawczy** and the office of **Naczelnik Państwa**, held by Józef Piłsudski. The transitional act of 18 May 1921 continued the Naczelnik's earlier powers pending the new presidency. There is no operating Senate or presidential officeholder at the opening. Piłsudski's state office is independent of the Piłsudczycy faction inside PPS.

**Antoni Ponikowski** leads a predominantly expert/administrative cabinet. This does not mean every minister was personally non-party. In this approved gameplay opening, PPS tolerates the cabinet from outside and holds no ministries. The exact historical basis for January PPS toleration remains **TBD — historical research required**.

Toleration is not executive control. You can organize PPS, campaign, use advisers and develop Milicja PPS, but cannot direct government taxation, appoint ministers or command the police through inherited Prussian powers. Cabinet portfolios can be inspected without joining the government. Adviser appointments do not appoint ministers.

The opening **444-MP** parliament is an approved simplification: rounded August 1922 club percentages are normalized and allocated as whole seats, not asserted to be the historical January roster. Existing party IDs stand for simplified parliamentary categories. Minority deputies are an aggregate, not a claim that a single minority electoral bloc already existed in January. Campaigning changes public support, not sitting MPs.

**Implemented parliamentary election:** November 1922, 444 Sejm MPs, a 223-MP majority and the approved national seat heuristic. ChZJN combines ZLN and PSChD votes and seats for display, while its constituent parties remain separate. Voting and government formation take place within one monthly sequence.

**Implemented presidential succession:** the December sequence uses the March Constitution's National Assembly procedure, fixed historical final ballots, Piłsudski's transfer to Narutowicz, Narutowicz's assassination and Wojciechowski's election. A proportional 111-seat Senate snapshot is created only for the 555-member Assembly; no general Senate mechanic exists. The approved compression omits the brief acting presidency. See the dedicated presidency page.

**Planned, not implemented:** subsequent presidential elections and historical cabinets. The next parliamentary date after November is temporarily May 1928; inherited events can alter later dates. Continuation elections use the same seat calculation, without pretending to implement later Polish chronology or alliances. There is no campaign cutoff.

**Approved election design:** the national vote-to-seat multipliers are 0.25 below 2%, 0.55 from 2%, 0.85 from 5%, 1.025 from 10%, 1.10 from 15%, and 1.25 from 25%. Weighted totals are normalized before whole seats are allocated by largest remainders. The user supplied/confirmed this calibration. These are gameplay rules, not the historical district law. There is no geographic model. Inne is split into 2% anonymous lists plus any smaller remainder, not counted as one electoral alliance. ChZJN seats are attributed to ZLN and PSChD proportionally to their support at the election.

@presidency
call: polish_opening_state
go-to: menu

= President of the Republic

Current head of state: **[+ head_of_state_display +]**.

Under the March Constitution, the Sejm and Senate elect the President together as the National Assembly for a seven-year term. Government acts require countersignature by the prime minister and responsible minister. The President appoints the prime minister and appoints other ministers on the prime minister's proposal. The office has no separate legislative veto or inherited German emergency-decree mechanic; dissolution of the Sejm requires the constitutionally specified Senate consent.

[? if not sejm_first_election_completed : The presidency does not operate at the January opening. The November Sejm election must take place before the December presidential sequence. ?]

[? if sejm_first_election_completed and not polish_first_presidential_election_recorded : The mandatory December 1922 presidential sequence has not yet begun. ?]

[? if polish_presidential_in_progress : The December sequence is in progress and will resume from its saved phase. ?]

[? if national_assembly_total > 0 : **Gameplay Assembly:** 444 Sejm MPs plus a proportional 111-seat Senate snapshot, for **[+ national_assembly_total +] members**. This does not implement a permanent Senate chamber. Fixed historical ballots are not recalculated from this simplified composition. ?]

[? if polish_first_presidential_election_recorded : **Final ballot, 9 December 1922:** Gabriel Narutowicz — 289 votes (PSL Wyzwolenie, PPS, most of PSL Piast, Blok Mniejszości Narodowych and part of NPR); Maurycy Zamoyski — 227 votes (ZLN and PSChD within ChZJN, with other right-wing parliamentarians). There were 29 blank ballots. Narutowicz was elected, took the oath on 11 December, and received the formal transfer of state authority from Józef Piłsudski on 14 December. ?]

[? if polish_first_presidential_election_recorded : **16 December:** Narutowicz was assassinated. PPS's peaceful constitutional response is recorded without numerical effects. The game omits the brief acting presidency as an approved chronology compression. ?]

[? if polish_second_presidential_election_recorded : **Final ballot, 20 December 1922:** Stanisław Wojciechowski — 298 votes (PSL Piast, PSL Wyzwolenie, PPS, NPR and Blok Mniejszości Narodowych); Kazimierz Morawski — 221 votes (ZLN and PSChD within ChZJN, with other right-wing parliamentarians). Wojciechowski was elected and assumed the presidency. ?]

Later Polish presidential elections, variable outcomes and their cabinet consequences remain planned. The inherited German direct-popular election and Hindenburg succession routes are unavailable under the Polish system.

@weimar_timeline
go-to: menu

= Temporary German-baseline timeline (until 1928)

**1875**: The SPD is formed as a merger of the General German Workers' Association, founded by Ferdinand Lassalle, and the Social Democratic Workers' Party of Germany, led by Wilhelm Liebknecht and August Bebel. Eventually, the SPD grows to become the largest party in the German Empire.

**1914**: The Great War begins. The SPD votes in favor of war credits, with substantial internal dissent. This begins a policy of *Burgfriedenspolitik*, a general peace between all political parties and trade unions.

**1917**: The USPD, a left-wing anti-war split from the SPD, is formed.

**1918**: Following defeats in the war and uprisings by soldiers, sailors, and workers, SPD leader Philip Scheidemann proclaims the German Republic, and Kaiser Wilhelm II abdicates from his throne. An armistice is declared, ending the Great War. Conflict between center-left social democrats and far-left radicals erupts in the streets of Berlin and elsewhere.

**1919**: The Spartacist Uprising, in which communist workers attempt to seize control of government, is launched. The uprising is defeated by the Social Democratic government, now led by President Friedrich Ebert and Chancellor Philipp Scheidemann, with the support of the far-right Freikorps militias. The two key leaders of the uprising, Rosa Luxemburg and Karl Liebknecht, are murdered by the Freikorps. The Treaty of Versailles is signed under great protest from all political factions in Germany, officially ending the Great War. The Weimar Constitution is signed. The first "Weimar Coalition" government is formed, led by the SPD, DDP, and Center (Z) parties.

**1920**: The Kapp Putsch, an attempt by reactionary actors within the military to take power, is launched, and is defeated by the workers with a general strike. This is followed by left-wing uprisings in the Ruhr, which are defeated by the Reichswehr and Freikorps. The Weimar Coalition is defeated in the elections, with the right and far left increasing in vote share. Elected governments in this period are very unstable, often only lasting a matter of months. There is a split in the USPD, with the majority faction joining with the KPD and the Soviet Union's Communist International (Comintern) alliance.

**1921**: Communist uprisings in Saxony and Hamburg are defeated. Matthias Erzberger, the Center Party leader who signed the Versailles Treaty, is assassinated.

**1922**: The Treaty of Rapallo is signed, which normalized relations with the Soviet Union. Walther Rathenau, the DDP Foreign Minister, is assassinated. The SPD reunites with most of the remaining USPD.

**1923**: Hyperinflation begins, largely due to the economic policy of "passive resistance" against the French occupation of the Ruhr and the Versailles reparations. The currency is rapidly devalued as prices for necessities rise to billions of Reichsmarks. The government launches a *Reichsexekution* against Saxony, which was led by a joint SPD-KPD government. The Nazi "Beer Hall Putsch" is launched in Munich, and fails, with the arrest of Hitler. Eventually, new policies manage to stabilize the currency, and the French occupation ends.

**1924**: Parliamentary elections are held in May and December, the latter leading to the bourgeois-right governments that last until 1928. Hitler is released from prison. The Dawes Plan for the repayment of the Versailles reparations is negotiated, which reduces payments and increases economic growth. Germany begins to enter a period of relative stability, with the end of the hyperinflation crisis. 

**1925**:  President Friedrich Ebert dies, and new presidential elections are held, leading to the narrow election of Paul von Hindenburg in the second round. Hindenburg is a war hero and a right-wing reactionary. The SPD adopts the Heidelberg Program as its party platform. The Locarno Treaties are signed, which confirmed Germany's post-war western borders.

**1926**: A referendum on the expropriation of the princes, supported by the SPD and KPD, fails.

